gful for  fratelli fertility guidelines unless the  soil pH is within the correct range and  remember that soil test results do not  always correlate with foliar test  results for a variety of reasons so you  may have adequate nutrition in the soil  while there appears not to be adequate  nutrition in the leaves or the reverse  may also be true you may appear to have  adequate nutrition and leaves but not in  soil tissue analysis tests are useful  for diagnosis but not for detailed  guidance unless you consider all of the  factors involved including growth and  yield and applying nutrients may result  in a decrease in foliar concentration  under certain circumstances correcting  deficiencies or imbalances and  established plantings is more difficult  than amending soils prior to planting so  really that pre-plan test and soil  amendment prior to planting is critical  and often provides enough nutrients  especially p and k and the micros for  the life of the planting  nitrogen is something that we need to  add annually and I mentioned that it's  often determined by the crop the plant  age irrigation and mulching status and  the leaf analysis results so often  growers will say okay my strawberry crop  is two years old and so I'm applying 25  pounds per acre of actual nitrogen and  if you actually used your leaf analysis  results in comparison to the crop age  you might find that you had sufficient  nitrogen and you might be able to apply  less or you might find that your  nitrogen is actually lacking and leaves  and you might need to apply more and for  more information on this topic you  should check the Cornell pest management  guidelines for Barry crops to find the  annual rates of nitrogen for your crop  or the N raise production guides for  more in-depth information on adjusting  these to get the proper results  so again in many cases no additional PK  mg or CA will be required if the soil  test recommendations were followed at  planting you may need to supplement k  and potassium and boron especially on  sandier soils because those have a  tendency to leach more quickly and small  amounts of sulfur may be required to  maintain a low pH in some soils where  blueberries are grown especially if  calcium levels are approaching 2000 leaf  analyses provides guidance on  supplemental fertilizers after the  planning established and don't rely on a  soil test for post plant recommendations  that do not involve soil pH remember the  pH has to be at the correct range  so in review don't rely on visual  symptoms of what you've always done in  the past in terms of very soil nutrient  management if you want to maximize your  yield and plant health before you plant  consider doing the Cornell soil health  test which includes the standard  chemical soil test and more the physical  and biological properties that Laura  discussed and then after plants are  established use annual tissue analysis  and soil testing every two to three  years to fine-tune your nutrient plan  that's the end of our presentation today  and we'd like to acknowledge dr. Marvin  prince who's the project leader of our  project he's a professor and chair of  the Department of Horticulture at  Cornell University you've already met  Laura Jeff and myself we'd also like to  thank Mario Miranda zazo one of our team  members whose with the tree fruit and  berry fruit extensions he's tree fruit  very extension specialist with cornell  extension lake ontario fruit team mr.  dan welch who's extension resource  educator with Cayuga County Cooperative  Extension dr. Harold Burness who's  professor in at Cornell University in  the Department of crop and soil sciences  who works very extensively with the  Cornell soil health program and mr.  Robert shindle back whose extension  associate also in the same department  and is the coordinator of the Coronel  soil health testing and we have special  thanks to mrs. Janet Fallon with Agri  one she's the certified crop adviser and  was very helpful in providing  information for this presentation thank  you  you
 

